¶
{{smaller block|His felawe hadde a staf ''tipped'' with horn.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Summoner's Tale, l. 32.}}}}

{{smaller block|That light, the breaking day, which ''tips''<br>The golden-spired Apocalypse!
{{right|''Whittier'', Chapel of the Hermits.}}}}

'''tip<sup>2</sup>''' (tip), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''tipped'', ppr. ''tipping''. [Early mod. E. also *''typpe, type''; < ME. ''tippen, tipen'', tip, overthrow, < Sw. ''tippa'', strike lightly, tap, tip, = LG. ''tippen'' = G. ''tüpfen, tupfen'', touch lightly, tap; appar. a secondary form, felt as a dim., of ''tap''<sup>2</sup>; but the relation with ''tap''<sup>2</sup> is uncertain.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To strike or hit lightly; tap.

{{smaller block|A third rogue ''tips'' me by the elbow.
{{right|''Swift'', Bickerstaff Papers.}}}}

'''2.''' To turn from a perpendicular position, as a solid object; cause to lean or slant; tilt; cant: usually implying but slight effort: as, to ''tip'' a bottle or a cart to discharge its contents; to ''tip'' a table or a chair.

{{smaller block|The red moon ''tipped''<br>Her horns athwart the tide.
{{right|''H. P. Spofford'', Poems, p. 98.}}}}

'''3'''†. To overthrow; overturn.

{{smaller block|''Type'' doun yonder toun.
{{right|''Alliterative Poems'' (ed. Morris), iii. 506.}}}}

'''4.''' To throw lightly to another; direct toward; give; communicate: as, to ''tip'' one a copper. [Slang.]

{{smaller block|''Tip'' the Captain one of your broadsides.
{{right|''Noctes Ambrosianæ'', Sept., 1832.}}}}

{{smaller block|"Egad," said Mr. Coverley, "the baronet has a mind to ''tip'' us a touch of the heroics this morning!"
{{right|''Miss Burney'', Evelina, lxxviii.}}}}
